Chinese open-weight models such as Qwen are a capable alternative to the closed
models of the major frontier labs. Their weights can be downloaded, run on your
own infrastructure and adapted to your needs. That makes them an important
building block for sovereign AI: more control over data, operations and further
development.

But a model you run yourself still carries the political framing of its
training. [Nous Research](https://nousresearch.com/arete) reports refusals,
evasive answers and Chinese state narratives in Qwen3.8-27B on politically
sensitive topics. Using such a model can therefore mean accepting behaviour
that conflicts with your own requirements. Can targeted post-training change
that behaviour?

This is what Nous Research investigated with **Arete-27B**. The team further
trained Qwen3.8-27B on around 52.8 million tokens, with the stated aim of
strengthening “Western values”. In its English evaluation, Nous reports a higher
factual score and substantially fewer propaganda flags after training. The
model, training approach and original **Propaganda_Eval** test come from
Nous Research.

Here, “propaganda” means adopting or endorsing Chinese state narratives under
this test's rubric. Merely reporting an official position with clear attribution
does not count.

But does a change like this carry over to another language? A better result in
English does not guarantee a better result in German. This is our research
question: **Does Arete's advantage over the original Qwen remain visible when
the same questions are asked in German?**

We translated all 803 Propaganda_Eval questions and tested Arete, Qwen, Kolibri
and Apertus in both languages. In the paired comparison with Qwen, Arete also
achieves a higher factual score and substantially fewer propaganda flags in
German. Both models score lower on factual coverage in German than in English.
That is the finding of this first run under the evaluation rubric we used.

We publish the [dataset](https://huggingface.co/datasets/namcheai/propaganda-eval-de)
and [methods, prompts and results](https://github.com/NamcheAI/propaganda-eval-de)
at [Namche](https://namche.ai). We did not train a model.

