[[eval:europe]]

European development and operating models on your own infrastructure are
important steps towards greater independence. But the same question arises:
what political framing does a model bring with it? Its training history is
part of the answer. Many models also learn from responses generated by other
AI systems. These **teacher models** supply synthetic training examples and can
pass on patterns of answering alongside capabilities.

**Kolibri 1** comes from Aleph Alpha in Heidelberg and was released in October
2026. It was pretrained from scratch for German and English, with around
78 billion parameters, of which around 3.5 billion are active per token. Its
[model card](https://huggingface.co/Aleph-Alpha/Kolibri-1) describes synthetic
rephrasing using Gemma and Mistral, among other methods. For post-training,
Aleph Alpha also reports synthetic data generated by Chinese language models. Its
[model card](https://huggingface.co/Aleph-Alpha/Kolibri-1#political-bias)
describes measures to reduce political bias inherited from these sources.

**Apertus** was developed through the Swiss AI Initiative at ETH Zurich and EPFL
with the Swiss National Supercomputing Centre, CSCS. We test the first-generation
8B-Instruct variant from September 2025. It was trained from scratch; weights,
data processing and training methods are openly documented. External models
also play a role here: according to the
[technical report](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.14233v1#S4.SS1.SSS4),
Llama 3.1 8B, Llama 3.3 70B, Qwen 2.5 72B, Qwen 3 14B and Qwen 3 32B generated
responses for alignment. On controversial topics, that alignment follows the
Swiss AI Charter. These details refer to the first generation tested here,
not the later Apertus 1.5.

The origin of teacher models alone does not establish inherited bias. That is
why we also measure these European models' actual behaviour in the same test.

`alephalpha/kolibri-1-tl5` and `publicai/apertus-8b-instruct` extend the comparison.
Questions, rubric, temperature and token limits match the other runs. They are
not control models for Arete's training: model families, sizes and serving
configurations differ. The proxy snapshots do not contain a verified immutable
weights revision for either model.

