## What the test measures

The [Nous Research test](https://nousresearch.com/arete) includes reference facts
and specific propaganda markers for politically sensitive questions. A judge
model evaluates answers against those criteria. The factual score ranges from
0 to 5 and captures which reference facts an answer includes, omits or
contradicts. It is not a general truthfulness rate. Propaganda here means a
flag under this rubric, not a comprehensive assessment of political neutrality.

The dataset comes with its own selection of topics and political framing.
Our translation preserves both. The questions were not rewritten for German,
Austrian or Swiss legal contexts.

For English, Nous reports a factual score of 2.98 and 0.9 percent propaganda
flags for Arete, compared with 2.32 points and 22 percent for Qwen. These are
averages across five runs. Our results come from one run per model and language;
missing judgments are not filled in as zeroes.

## Translate first, then review

GPT-6 Astra produced the translation with reasoning set to high. Claude Fable
5.1 reviewed it against the original at medium. The prompts, model parameters
and additional provider or client system instructions are in the repository.

All 670 review points have documented decisions: 39 by humans, 617 through
commissioned case-by-case AI review and 14 under the agreed number-formatting
rule. A separate grammatical correction is also documented. For example,
English `1,234.56` becomes German `1.234,56`, preserving the value.

The approach draws on the [MQM framework](https://themqm.org/) and
[Datasheets for Datasets](https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09010). It combines human
and AI review; it is not complete independent human validation or MQM
certification. The [methodology](https://github.com/NamcheAI/propaganda-eval-de/blob/main/METHOD.md)
describes the steps and limitations.

## The same test in two languages

Each model received the same 803 questions in English and German, at temperature
0 and with a maximum of 32,768 output tokens. Arete ran in BF16 on an RTX PRO
6000 with 96 GB, using vLLM 0.32.0, a pinned weights revision, a 65,536-token
context and thinking disabled. It received no additional system message.

Qwen3.8-27B was accessed through OpenRouter and the Namche inference proxy.
Thinking was disabled with `reasoning.enabled=false`; stored token statistics
provide a check. OpenRouter documents its model and provider endpoints,
including quantisation. That public description is distinct from the actual
endpoint selected for an individual request. Qwen and Arete did not run on
identical serving infrastructure, so the comparison does not isolate the
Arete training alone.

For all four models, Grok 4.7 judged the answers using the original English
rubric, temperature 0 and a maximum of 4,096 output tokens. Questions, reference
facts and propaganda markers remained in English for the judge; only the
language of the answer being evaluated changed. Its cross-language judging
quality has not been independently calibrated.

Missing calls and calls with technical or formal failures were retried
selectively. Successful answers and valid judgments were preserved. Earlier
attempts and remaining failures stay documented. Factual scores, fact mappings
and evidence quotes must pass the unchanged validation. For remaining formal
errors, the same judge received its previous judgment and
[standardised validation feedback](https://github.com/NamcheAI/propaganda-eval-de/blob/main/prompts/judge-validation-repair.md).
The rubric and model parameters stayed the same; originals and corrections
are stored separately. The downloads also include the analysis without this
step. It covers $baseline_n common Arete-Qwen cases and likewise shows a higher
German factual score and fewer propaganda flags for Arete.

## Limits of this first run

There is one run per model and language, with no estimate of variation across
repeated runs. $remaining
Translation errors and language-dependent judge effects remain possible. The
numbers measure reference facts and markers under this rubric, not general
truthfulness, political neutrality or suitability for German, Austrian or
Swiss law.

